  "text": "She came for water at the worst hour of the day and left as the first person to go preach him.\n\nJohn four. Jesus is tired, sits down by a well, and it's about the sixth hour. Midday. And a woman of Samaria comes to draw water.\n\nHe says, \"Give me to drink.\"\n\nScandal. Two of them. She says it herself: \"How is it that thou, being a Jew, askest drink of me, which am a woman of Samaria?\"\n\nThen the conversation turns. He offers her living water. She wants it. He says go call your husband.\n\nAnd she says, \"I have no husband.\"\n\nAnd he goes, \"Thou hast well said. For thou hast had five husbands, and he whom thou now hast is not thy husband.\"\n\nHe's not dragging her. He's telling her he already knows and he's still sitting there.\n\nNow, people love to say she came at noon to dodge the gossip. That is a reasonable guess. It is still a guess. The text just says sixth hour.\n\nWhat the text does say is this. She left her waterpot. Left it. And went into the city and told the men, \"Come, see a man which told me all things that ever I did.\"\n\nShe dropped the jar and became the testimony.",
